# 3. Areas of Clinical Focus (NCE content outline, 29%): 56 items
# Covers the outline's client concerns plus the CACREP areas behind them: human growth and
# development, career development, and social and cultural diversity.
CAT = "nceClinical"; SEG = "CLN"
Q = [
# Development
("A 45-year-old client wants to mentor younger coworkers and leave something lasting. In Erikson's theory, she is working on:",
 "Generativity vs. stagnation", ["Ego integrity vs. despair", "Intimacy vs. isolation", "Industry vs. inferiority"],
 "Generativity vs. stagnation is the task of middle adulthood: guiding the next generation and contributing to the world. Integrity vs. despair comes in late life."),
("A 78-year-old client reviews his life and feels it had meaning. Erikson would call this resolution:",
 "Integrity", ["Generativity", "Identity", "Autonomy"],
 "Ego integrity vs. despair is the final stage, in which older adults look back and accept their life as meaningful. Despair is the unresolved outcome."),
("A 4-year-old insists the tall glass holds more juice than the short wide glass with the same amount. Piaget would say the child has not yet mastered:",
 "Conservation", ["Object permanence", "Abstract reasoning", "Egocentric speech"],
 "Conservation, knowing an amount stays the same when its shape changes, develops in the concrete operational stage. A 4-year-old is in the preoperational stage."),
("At which Piaget stage do adolescents begin to reason about hypothetical possibilities?",
 "Formal operational", ["Concrete operational", "Preoperational", "Sensorimotor"],
 "Formal operational thinking, beginning around age 11 or 12, allows abstract and hypothetical reasoning. Concrete operational thinking is tied to real objects."),
("A teen says stealing is wrong because you might get caught and punished. Kohlberg would place this reasoning at the:",
 "Preconventional level", ["Conventional level", "Postconventional level", "Social contract stage"],
 "Preconventional reasoning judges right and wrong by punishment and reward. Conventional reasoning follows social rules and approval."),
("Carol Gilligan's work on moral development emphasized:",
 "An ethic of care", ["Stages of cognition", "Defense mechanisms", "Birth order effects"],
 "Gilligan argued that Kohlberg's justice-based stages missed an ethic of care and relationships, which she found prominent in women's moral reasoning."),
("In the Strange Situation, an infant is distressed when the parent leaves and is quickly comforted on return. This pattern is:",
 "Secure attachment", ["Avoidant attachment", "Resistant attachment", "Disorganized attachment"],
 "Ainsworth found securely attached infants protest separation and are readily soothed at reunion. Resistant infants stay upset and push away even while seeking contact."),
("A college student has explored many career and value options but has not yet committed to any. Marcia would call this:",
 "Moratorium", ["Foreclosure", "Identity achievement", "Identity diffusion"],
 "Moratorium is active exploration without commitment. Foreclosure is commitment without exploration, often to parents' choices."),
# Career
("A client's top Holland code is Social. Which work setting would she most likely prefer?",
 "Teaching or helping others", ["Repairing machines outdoors", "Analyzing lab data alone", "Managing a sales team"],
 "In Holland's RIASEC model, Social types like helping, teaching and serving people. Realistic types prefer hands-on work with tools and machines."),
("In Holland's hexagon, which pair of types is most similar?",
 "Realistic and Investigative", ["Realistic and Social", "Artistic and Conventional", "Investigative and Enterprising"],
 "Adjacent types on the hexagon are most alike. Realistic sits next to Investigative, while Realistic and Social are opposite each other."),
("Super's life-span, life-space theory describes career development through stages and the idea of:",
 "Implementing a self-concept", ["Matching traits to job factors", "Narrowing choices by sex type", "Learning from chance events"],
 "Super saw career choice as implementing a self-concept across stages: growth, exploration, establishment, maintenance and disengagement. Trait-factor matching is Parsons's approach."),
("Krumboltz's happenstance learning theory encourages clients to:",
 "Make use of unplanned events", ["Pick one goal and never change it", "Rely only on aptitude tests", "Wait for the ideal job"],
 "Planned happenstance teaches curiosity, persistence, flexibility, optimism and risk taking so clients can turn chance events into opportunities."),
("Gottfredson's theory explains how young people narrow their career options through:",
 "Circumscription and compromise", ["Differentiation and fusion", "Assimilation and accommodation", "Exploration and maintenance"],
 "Gottfredson says children rule out jobs that seem wrong for their sex type or social class (circumscription), then give up preferred options for accessible ones (compromise)."),
("Frank Parsons, an early figure in career guidance, matched people to jobs using:",
 "Self-knowledge and job knowledge", ["Dream analysis and insight", "Family sculpting and roles", "Schedules of token reinforcement"],
 "Parsons's trait-and-factor approach asked people to understand themselves, learn about jobs and reason about the match between the two."),
("A laid-off 58-year-old client is unsure how to cope with leaving work. Schlossberg's transition model would assess his:",
 "Situation, self, support, strategies", ["Id, ego and superego conflicts", "Realistic and social interest codes", "Stages of his moral reasoning"],
 "Schlossberg's 4 S model looks at the situation, the self, the supports and the strategies a person brings to a transition."),
("Social cognitive career theory stresses the role of:",
 "Self-efficacy beliefs", ["Early childhood conflicts", "Unconscious drives", "Birth order"],
 "Social cognitive career theory, from Lent, Brown and Hackett, links career interests and choices to self-efficacy beliefs, outcome expectations and personal goals."),
# Multicultural
("In Berry's model, an immigrant who loses touch with her heritage culture and also feels shut out of the new one is experiencing:",
 "Marginalization", ["Integration", "Separation", "Assimilation"],
 "Marginalization is low contact with both the heritage culture and the host culture, the acculturation strategy most linked to stress. Separation keeps the heritage culture while avoiding the new one."),
("A client describes coworkers' repeated small remarks that imply he does not belong. These are best described as:",
 "Microaggressions", ["Cultural idioms", "Acculturation stress", "Overt hate crimes"],
 "Microaggressions are everyday slights, often unintended, that send hostile or demeaning messages about a person's group membership."),
("In Cross's model of Black racial identity, a person whose worldview is shaken by an experience of racism is in the:",
 "Encounter stage", ["Pre-encounter stage", "Internalization stage", "Integrative awareness stage"],
 "In Cross's nigrescence model, the encounter stage follows an event that challenges a person's earlier views on race and starts a search for identity."),
("A Mexican American client describes her nervous distress as ataque de nervios. A counselor should recognize this as a:",
 "Cultural concept of distress", ["Psychotic symptom", "Sign of malingering", "Trait of a personality disorder"],
 "DSM-5-TR lists ataque de nervios as a cultural concept of distress: a culturally shaped way of expressing suffering that should be understood in context."),
("Which describes an etic approach to multicultural counseling?",
 "Looking for what is universal", ["Looking at one culture's own view", "Matching clients by race", "Avoiding all talk of culture"],
 "An etic view looks for shared, universal aspects of human experience. An emic view focuses on what is specific to a particular culture."),
# Grief and loss
("Worden's four tasks of mourning include:",
 "Adjusting to life without the deceased", ["Passing through denial before anger", "Reaching closure within one year", "Avoiding the pain of the loss"],
 "Worden's tasks are to accept the reality of the loss, process the pain, adjust to a world without the deceased and find an enduring connection while moving on."),
("A client grieves the death of an ex-partner, but friends and family do not acknowledge her loss. This is:",
 "Disenfranchised grief", ["Anticipatory grief", "Prolonged grief disorder", "Normal bereavement"],
 "Disenfranchised grief is loss that is not openly acknowledged or socially supported, such as the death of an ex-partner, a pet or a secret relationship."),
("Under DSM-5-TR, prolonged grief disorder in an adult requires that at least how long has passed since the death?",
 "12 months", ["2 weeks", "6 months", "24 months"],
 "DSM-5-TR added prolonged grief disorder, which requires at least 12 months since the death for adults and at least 6 months for children and adolescents."),
("A wife whose husband has advanced dementia mourns the losses before his death. This is called:",
 "Anticipatory grief", ["Delayed grief", "Complicated grief", "Chronic grief"],
 "Anticipatory grief is mourning that begins before an expected death, common among caregivers of people with terminal or degenerative illness."),
# Mood and anxiety
("A client has depressed mood most days for the past two and a half years without a break longer than two months. This suggests:",
 "Persistent depressive disorder", ["Major depressive disorder", "Cyclothymic disorder", "Adjustment disorder"],
 "Persistent depressive disorder requires depressed mood for most of the day, more days than not, for at least two years in adults, without symptom-free periods longer than two months."),
("For a diagnosis of a manic episode in bipolar I disorder, symptoms must last at least:",
 "One week", ["Four days", "Two weeks", "One month"],
 "A manic episode lasts at least one week, or any length if hospitalization is needed. A hypomanic episode lasts at least four consecutive days."),
("A client has numerous periods of hypomanic and depressive symptoms over two years, none meeting full episode criteria. This suggests:",
 "Cyclothymic disorder", ["Bipolar II disorder", "Bipolar I disorder", "Major depressive disorder"],
 "Cyclothymic disorder involves at least two years of fluctuating hypomanic and depressive symptoms that never meet the criteria for a full episode."),
("A client has recurrent unexpected surges of intense fear with chest pain, then a month of worry about the next one. This suggests:",
 "Panic disorder", ["Generalized anxiety disorder", "Specific phobia", "Illness anxiety disorder"],
 "Panic disorder requires recurrent unexpected panic attacks followed by at least a month of persistent worry about more attacks or a change in behavior."),
("Excessive worry about many everyday matters for at least six months, with restlessness and poor sleep, suggests:",
 "Generalized anxiety disorder", ["Panic disorder", "Social anxiety disorder", "Adjustment disorder with anxiety"],
 "Generalized anxiety disorder is hard-to-control worry about several areas of life, more days than not, for at least six months, with physical symptoms such as restlessness and sleep problems."),
("A client spends hours checking that the stove is off and feels great anxiety if he does not. Checking is best described as a:",
 "Compulsion", ["Obsession", "Delusion", "Specific phobia"],
 "Compulsions are repeated behaviors done to reduce anxiety caused by obsessions. The intrusive thought that the stove might be on is the obsession."),
("Which physical symptom is commonly linked to depression?",
 "Changes in appetite and sleep", ["A persistent high fever", "Sudden loss of hearing in one ear", "A rash on both hands"],
 "Depression often shows in the body through appetite and weight changes, sleep changes, fatigue and slowed movement."),
# Trauma, abuse and violence
("A client avoids reminders of an assault, has nightmares and is easily startled four months later. Which diagnosis fits best?",
 "Posttraumatic stress disorder", ["Acute stress disorder", "Specific phobia, situational", "Adjustment disorder with anxiety"],
 "PTSD involves intrusion, avoidance, negative changes in mood and thinking, and arousal lasting more than a month after a trauma."),
("A counselor has a reasonable suspicion, but no proof, that a 7-year-old client is being abused at home. What must she do?",
 "Report the suspicion as state law requires", ["Gather proof before she reports", "Ask the parents to explain first", "Promise the child to keep it secret"],
 "Counselors are mandated reporters of suspected child abuse in every state. The duty is triggered by reasonable suspicion, not proof, and investigation is left to child protective services."),
("In Walker's cycle of violence, the phase after an abusive incident in which the abuser apologizes and is affectionate is:",
 "Honeymoon phase", ["Tension-building phase", "Acute battering phase", "Separation phase"],
 "Walker's cycle runs from tension building to the acute battering incident to loving contrition, often called the honeymoon phase, before tension builds again."),
("A client in an abusive relationship is planning to leave. What should the counselor help her develop?",
 "A safety plan", ["A couples counseling plan", "A confrontation script", "A forgiveness letter"],
 "Leaving is often the most dangerous time. A safety plan covers safe places, documents, supports and emergency steps. Couples counseling is generally not advised while abuse is ongoing."),
# Substance use and process addictions
("Which behavioral addiction is listed as a diagnosis in the DSM-5-TR?",
 "Gambling disorder", ["Internet gaming disorder", "Shopping addiction", "Pornography addiction"],
 "Gambling disorder is the only non-substance addiction in the DSM-5-TR main text. Internet gaming disorder is a condition for further study."),
("Needing more and more of a drug to get the same effect is called:",
 "Tolerance", ["Withdrawal", "Craving", "Remission"],
 "Tolerance is a reduced effect from the same amount, or a need for markedly more to get the effect. Withdrawal is the syndrome that follows stopping."),
("Which substance's withdrawal can be life-threatening and usually needs medical supervision?",
 "Alcohol", ["Cannabis", "Caffeine", "Nicotine"],
 "Alcohol withdrawal can bring seizures and delirium tremens, so medically supervised detox is advised. Cannabis and nicotine withdrawal are uncomfortable but rarely dangerous."),
# Eating, sleep and other concerns
("A client restricts food, has a very low weight and an intense fear of gaining weight. This suggests:",
 "Anorexia nervosa", ["Bulimia nervosa", "Binge-eating disorder", "Pica"],
 "Anorexia nervosa involves restriction leading to significantly low weight, fear of weight gain and a disturbed body image."),
("A client has recurrent binges with a sense of loss of control but no purging or other compensating behavior. This suggests:",
 "Binge-eating disorder", ["Bulimia nervosa", "Anorexia nervosa, binge type", "Rumination disorder"],
 "Binge-eating disorder has recurrent binges with distress but no regular compensatory behavior such as vomiting. Bulimia nervosa includes compensatory behaviors."),
("Which advice is part of sleep hygiene for a client with insomnia?",
 "Keep a regular wake time", ["Nap whenever tired", "Use the bed to read and work", "Exercise right before bed"],
 "Sleep hygiene includes a consistent wake time, using the bed only for sleep and sex, limiting naps and caffeine, and avoiding vigorous exercise close to bedtime."),
("A client hears voices commenting on her actions when no one is present. This symptom is an:",
 "Auditory hallucination", ["Perceptual illusion", "Persecutory delusion of being watched", "Idea of reference"],
 "A hallucination is a perception without a stimulus. A delusion is a fixed false belief, and an illusion is a misperception of a real stimulus."),
("Psychotic symptoms that have lasted four months, with no prior episodes and no mood disorder, suggest:",
 "Schizophreniform disorder", ["Brief psychotic disorder", "Schizophrenia", "Schizoaffective disorder, bipolar type"],
 "Schizophreniform disorder lasts at least one month but less than six. Brief psychotic disorder lasts under a month, and schizophrenia requires at least six months."),
("A 9-year-old has a persistent pattern of inattention and hyperactivity at home and school. For ADHD, symptoms must have been present before age:",
 "12", ["7", "5", "18"],
 "DSM-5-TR requires several symptoms of inattention or hyperactivity-impulsivity before age 12, present in two or more settings."),
("A client's distress about the mismatch between his experienced gender and assigned sex is significant and lasting. The counselor should:",
 "Affirm his identity, explore distress", ["Try to change his gender identity", "Treat the identity as the problem", "Refer him only for medical care at once"],
 "Affirming practice respects the client's gender identity and works on the distress, supports and goals the client brings. Efforts to change identity are harmful and rejected by professional bodies."),
# Family, couples and life stage
("A remarried couple argues because the stepfather tries to discipline the children right away. A counselor would most likely suggest:",
 "The birth parent handles discipline first", ["The stepparent takes over discipline", "Neither parent sets any firm limits", "The children move to the other home"],
 "Blended family research suggests the biological parent leads discipline early on while the stepparent builds a warm relationship before taking on that role."),
("Which is a common reaction of school-age children to their parents' divorce?",
 "Believing they caused it", ["Showing no reaction at all", "Accepting it right away", "Wishing for more changes"],
 "Children often blame themselves or hope their parents will reunite. Reassuring them that the divorce is not their fault is a common counseling goal."),
("A couple keeps repeating a cycle in which one partner criticizes and the other withdraws. This pattern is often called:",
 "Pursue-withdraw", ["Enmeshment", "Triangulation", "Scapegoating"],
 "In a pursue-withdraw cycle, one partner seeks connection through criticism or demands and the other shuts down, which leads to more pursuit."),
("An adoptee in her 20s wants to search for her birth parents. The counselor's best stance is to:",
 "Help her explore the search", ["Discourage any search at all", "Contact her birth parents for her", "Tell her adoptive parents first"],
 "Questions about origins are common for adoptees. The counselor helps the client explore her hopes, fears and supports and decide whether and how to search."),
("A daughter caring for her father with Parkinson's disease reports exhaustion and resentment. These signs most suggest:",
 "Caregiver burden", ["Personality disorder", "Malingering", "Hypomanic episode"],
 "Caregiver burden includes physical exhaustion, resentment, guilt and isolation. Counselors address respite, support and the caregiver's own needs."),
("A recently retired client feels lost without his work role. A counselor would focus on:",
 "Building new roles and routines", ["Finding a full-time job at once", "Avoiding talk about the change", "Diagnosing a mood disorder"],
 "Retirement is a major role transition. Counseling helps the client find new sources of meaning, structure and social connection."),
("A client facing a terminal diagnosis asks about the meaning of her life. These are best described as:",
 "Existential concerns", ["Psychotic symptoms", "Career concerns", "Cognitive distortions"],
 "Questions of meaning, death, freedom and isolation are existential concerns, common in serious illness and end-of-life care."),
("A child is the target of repeated cruel texts from classmates. The counselor's first priority is to:",
 "Assess the child's safety", ["Tell the child to ignore them", "Call the classmates' parents", "Take away the child's phone"],
 "Cyberbullying is linked to depression and suicidal thoughts. The counselor first assesses safety and distress, then works with the family and school on a plan."),
("A 6-year-old refuses school, has stomachaches and fears her mother will be hurt whenever they are apart, for two months. This suggests:",
 "Separation anxiety disorder", ["Specific phobia", "Social anxiety disorder", "Reactive attachment disorder"],
 "Separation anxiety disorder is fear about separation from attachment figures beyond what is expected for the child's age, lasting at least four weeks in children, often with worry that harm will come to the parent."),
("A college student in a new country feels homesick, out of place and confused by social norms. This is often called:",
 "Culture shock", ["Identity diffusion", "Social anxiety disorder", "Agoraphobia"],
 "Culture shock is the disorientation that follows moving into an unfamiliar culture. It often eases as the person adapts."),
]
